.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd October 13, 2026
.Dt E1000 4
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm e1000
.Nd Intel 82540EM Ethernet driver
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver in the kernel drives one Intel 82540EM network controller (PCI
vendor 8086, device 100e), the model QEMU emulates as
.Ql -device e1000 .
It is probed only when the boot command line carries the
.Ql net
option; every other boot leaves network devices untouched.
.Pp
The driver resets the controller, masks all its interrupts, reads the MAC
address from receive address 0 (refusing a controller that does not mark
it valid), and waits a bounded time for link up.
A link that is not up by then is not a refusal: the controller is given
out with the link down, the link flag is read live on every
.Ql nic_info ,
and frames flow once carrier returns with nothing re-probed.
It keeps one receive ring of 32 and one transmit ring of 16 descriptors,
each with a 2 KiB buffer in kernel memory, and polls them on the kernel's
tick; it uses no interrupts.
.Pp
The kernel moves whole Ethernet frames, 14 to 1514 bytes, and nothing
else.
Exactly one process holds the network card's handle: in the qualified
boot, the SSH server.
The TCP/IP stack runs inside that process (smoltcp): static IPv4 only,
10.0.2.15/24 with gateway 10.0.2.2, answering ARP, ICMP echo, and TCP.
There are no kernel sockets, no IPv6, UDP, DNS, or DHCP, and no outbound
client on the system.
.Pp
Everything the device writes is treated as untrusted.
A received frame is delivered only if its descriptor is complete, holds the
whole frame, reports no error, and has a length in range; anything else is
counted as refused and dropped.
If the device moves a ring pointer outside its ring, or a send does not
complete in time while the link is up, the driver disables the
controller: receiving and sending stop, and every later request fails.
A send that does not complete in time while the link is down, or with a
loss of carrier seen during its wait, is a link outcome, not a failure:
the request returns the no-carrier error
.Pq Li -100 ,
the controller stays enabled, and the descriptor stays the device's;
later sends are refused at once until it completes or carrier returns.
With carrier back and the descriptor still not complete, the next send
waits for it once more; if that wait runs out with carrier present
throughout, the driver disables the controller.
A device that fails across a loss of carrier is therefore reported as
the link outcome first, and as a failure only at the next send whose wait
runs with carrier present throughout, or never if nothing more is sent.
A loss shorter than the driver's poll interval can be missed, and the send
then disables the controller as above.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
On the serial console:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql NIC CONTROLLER pci=BB:DD.F vendor=8086 device=100e mac=...
the controller was found.
.It Ql NETWORK LINK OK rx_descriptors=32 tx_descriptors=16 buffer_bytes=2048
the link is up and the rings are ready.
.It Ql NIC LINK DOWN AT PROBE rx_descriptors=32 tx_descriptors=16 buffer_bytes=2048
the rings are ready and the controller is given out, with no carrier yet.
.It Ql NIC UNAVAILABLE Ar reason
no usable controller; nothing is given out and a
.Ql net
boot fails.
.It Ql NIC DISABLED reason= Ns Ar reason
the driver stopped the controller (fail closed).
In a served boot sshd then ends with
.Ql SERVED SERVICE FAULT component=sshd reason=nic_disabled
and the machine is reset under the recovery budget; the next boot's probe
re-admits the controller or refuses it before readiness.
.It Ql NIC TX HOLD FIXTURE ARMED mode=release|stuck kind=tctl
qualification boots only: the kernel holds a transmit descriptor posted
without carrier, standing in for a device that does; refused in the
physical profile, never in an operator boot.
.It Ql NIC STATS rx_frames=... rx_refused=... rx_missed=... tx_frames=...
counters at the end of the boot.
.El
.Sh QUALIFICATION
QEMU-qualified on the 82540EM model
.Pq Ql -device e1000
by the platform's network, SSH, and file server matrices.
QEMU's device completes a send with the link down, so the no-carrier rule
is shown on QEMU only through the kernel's transmit-hold qualification
fixture, which demonstrates the driver's rule, not the device's.
Physical hardware qualification: none; no physical controller is
qualified for the no-carrier rule until it is observed on the named
machine.
The Ethernet controllers in current laptops (Intel I219, I225) are
different devices and are not supported.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr afterboot 8
.Pp
In the kernel repository:
.Pa docs/NETWORK.md .
.Sh CAVEATS
Polling on the tick adds up to a millisecond of receive latency, and a
burst faster than 32 descriptors drain between ticks is dropped (counted as
.Ql rx_missed ;
TCP resends it).
No multicast filters, checksum or segmentation offload, or VLANs.
The address is compiled in; it cannot be configured yet.
